             \end{steps}



\section*{Calculations}
\mycircled{0} The mass of KCl your weighted, $m$.\vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{1} The average activity in cpm.\vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{2} The standard deviation in cpm. \vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{3} These are the activity values in cpm for the background (in the absence of KCl).\vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{4} These are the activity values in cpm for the sample when KCl is present.\vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{5} These are the activity (standard deviation) values in cpm for the sample without the background. You can subtract the average value and the standard deviation.
\[  \;\;\mycircled{5} \;\;\;\; \mycircled{1} \;\;\ = \;\;\mycircled{4} \;\;\;\; - \;\;\;\; \mycircled{3} \;\;\  \]
For the standard deviation ($s_{s}$), you need to subtract the squared standard deviation of the background ($s_{b}$) from the squared standard deviation of the sample with background ($s_{b+s}$) and take the square root of the result
\[ \;\; \mycircled{5} \;\;\;\; \mycircled{2} \;\;\ =s_s=\sqrt{s_{b+s}^2-s_{b}^2} = \sqrt{\;\;\mycircled{4} \;\;\;\; \mycircled{2} \;\;\;\; - \;\;\;\; \mycircled{3} \;\;\;\; \mycircled{2}  }  \] 
\vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{6} These are the activity per mass values in logarithmic form
\[  log(  \;\;\mycircled{5} \;\;\  / \;\;\mycircled{0} \;\;\       )\]
\mycircled{7} Use your graphing calculator or Excel to adjust a linear plot where Y would be log($\frac{A}{m}$) and X would be m. Obtain the y-intercept, the slope, and the $r^2$ value. \vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{8} Obtain the maximum value for $\frac{A}{m}$ by doing an antilogarithm of the Y-intercept
\[  Max(\frac{A}{m})=10^{Y-intercept}        \]
\vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{9} We can now calculate the half-life (in years) of the isotope
\[ t^{calc.}_{1/2} (LR)= \frac{0.693}{\;\;\mycircled{8} \;\;\ }\times   \frac{\epsilon}{100}\times\frac{9.53\times 10^{17}}{525600}\]
where $\epsilon$ is the efficiency of the Geiger counter (usually 20\%). The number 525600 is used to convert minutes to years, whereas the number $9.53\times 10^{17}\text{K-40 atoms}/\text{g KCl}$ is used to convert from K-40 atoms into KCl grams. \vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{10} This is the half-life of K-40 as reported in the literature\vspace{0.5cm}\\
%\mycircled{11} This is the measured activity of the isotope\vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{11} This is the  date of the isotopes listed on the chip\vspace{0.5cm}\\
\mycircled{12} This is the time passed from when the activity listed on the chip was measured\vspace{0.5cm}\\
%\mycircled{13} These are the activity (standard deviation) values in cpm for the sample without the background. You can subtract the average value and the standard deviation.
