\mycircled{13} This is the efficiency of the counter
\[ \epsilon= \frac{\;\;\mycircled{4} \;\;\ - \;\;\mycircled{3} \;\;\ }{A_{ref}\cdot 0.5^{ \frac{ t }{t_{\frac{1}{2}}} }     }  \times   \frac{100}{2.22\times 10^{6}} \cdot \frac{1}{f} \]
where $A_{ref}$ is the activity of the source in $\mu$Ci as listed on its label (0.1$\mu$ Ci for all samples), $t$ is the number of years passed from the date written on the chip, and $t_{\frac{1}{2}}$ is the half-life of the isotope in years too. The number $2.22\times 10^{6}\text{cpm}/\mu Ci$ is used to convert from $\mu Ci$ to cpm. The factor $f$ is the geometric efficiency of the counter (here equals to 0.3) and takes into account geometric facts (distance $d$=15mm and counter window diameter $x$=35mm) that make it so that not all disintegrations reach the counter. It is the fraction between the area of the top cap of the sphere with the source on its center ($2\pi r\cdot h$, that is the radiation received) and area of the sphere with the shource on its center ($4\pi\cdot r^2$)
\[  f = \frac{2\pi r\cdot h }{4\pi\cdot r^2}=\frac{h}{2r} =\frac{r-d}{2r} =\frac{1}{2} - \frac{d}{2\sqrt{ x^2+d^2  }} \]
